Z03-P13 | Balance heat, fuel and oxygen in a controlled small fire | Smokey Bear guide and USFS fire safety; new manuscript
objective: Manage ignition energy, dry fuel sizing and open airflow so an authorized small fire burns cleanly without escalating.
applies: Lawful outdoor campfires in approved rings during permitted weather conditions.
equipment: Pre-staged dry tinder, small kindling, modest deadwood fuel, water and stirring tool.
action: Recognize that sustained combustion requires all three elements together—sufficient heat, dry finely divided fuel and unobstructed oxygen flow—removing any one stops the flame. @C03,C04,ED
action: Keep initial flame small and open to air rather than piling thick logs tightly onto tiny sparks, which suffocates oxygen and chills the ignition point. @C03,C17
action: Maintain a modest manageable fire size suited to cooking or brief warmth; oversized fires multiply ember loft, radiant hazard and water needed for full extinguishment. @C02,C04,C17
failure: Smothering a weak tinder flame with damp thick sticks or stacking high logs that throw sparks outside the fire ring. @C02,C03,C17
verify: Confirm steady small flame with clean upward draft, zero overhanging branches and extinguishing water within arm's reach. @C02,C04,ED
stop: If wind gusts scatter sparks, fuel burns too large to manage or smoke blows across shelters, drown and stir the fire cold immediately. @C02,C04,C18
diagram: Heat + dry fuel + airflow | Build gradually from fine tinder | Keep small & controllable
visual: Mechanism diagram illustrating balanced heat, graduated dry fuel and convective oxygen intake within a lawful fire ring. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and combustion safety

Z03-P14 | Clear a lawful fire site down to mineral soil | USFS campfire safety and Smokey Bear guide; new manuscript
objective: Remove flammable needles, grass, leaves and organic duff before starting an authorized ground fire.
applies: Permitted outdoor campfires where an established metal or stone fire ring is required or lawful ground sites are allowed.
equipment: Metal fire ring where required, clearing tool, extinguishing water and non-combustible base.
action: Confirm open fires are legally permitted for the exact date and land unit, preferring an established fire ring whenever available or required. @C01,C02,C20
action: Clear dry grass, pine needles, leaves, twigs and loose organic duff away from the fire perimeter down to bare mineral soil, and scan overhead for low branches. @C14,C02
action: Inspect the cleared soil for buried tree roots or deep spongy peat; organic subsurface layers can ignite silently and creep beyond the visible ring. @C14,C04
failure: Building a fire directly on thick pine duff or dry forest leaf litter because the surface looks damp after morning dew. @C14,C02
verify: Confirm bare mineral soil around the burn area, clear overhead canopy and ready water before striking any ignition source. @C14,C02,C04,ED
stop: Reject sites with deep peat, exposed tree roots, dry tall grass or overhanging limbs; use a permitted stove or no-fire warmth instead. @C14,C01,C08
diagram: Clear grass & needles away | Expose bare mineral soil | Exclude roots, peat & limbs
visual: Cutaway ground profile contrasting safe bare mineral soil against flammable surface needles, duff and subsurface root networks. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Wildland fire prevention and land management

Z03-P15 | Separate dry deadwood interior from wet or rotted bark | B012 P20 winter tinder and Smokey Bear guide; adapted historical material
objective: Test fuel dryness inside the wood rather than judging by outer bark appearance after rain or snow.
applies: Preparing lawful dead and downed wood for an authorized outdoor fire in damp or winter conditions.
equipment: Lawfully gathered dead downed wood, hand inspection and safe cutting tool if equipped and trained.
action: Reject spongy, crumbling, punky or water-logged ground wood that bends or dampens to the touch—wet rot steams and extinguishes weak flames. @C03,C17,ED
action: Check dead small twigs and split sound deadwood cores where lawful; rain and snow often wet only the outer bark while leaving the sound interior fiber dry. @C03,ED
action: Snap-test fine twigs cleanly (crisp snap versus dull damp bend) and store prepared dry interior material off wet ground before attempting ignition. @C03,ED
failure: Gathering damp surface sticks off soggy moss and mistaking dry-looking outer bark for dry interior kindling. @C03,ED
verify: Confirm tinder and small kindling snap crisply, feel dry against skin and stay isolated from wet snow or soil. @C03,ED
stop: If available wood is saturated throughout and dry material cannot be staged safely, stop spending energy on fire and shift to no-fire insulation. @C03,C08
diagram: Wet outer bark or snow | Sound dry interior fiber | Reject spongy rotted wood
visual: Material cross-section contrasting rain-wetted outer bark and rotted ground wood against dry sound interior wood fibers. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and field education

Z03-P16 | Gather dead and downed wood lawfully without damaging live trees | NPS Leave No Trace and Minnesota DNR park rules; new manuscript
objective: Source modest wood fuel responsibly where permitted while respecting park bans and forest-pest rules.
applies: Public lands where local regulations explicitly allow gathering dead and downed wood for campfires.
equipment: Current park/forest rules, gloves if available and lawful established fire ring.
action: Check whether wood gathering is permitted in the specific park or wilderness unit; many state parks prohibit collecting any wood and require approved or certified firewood. @C20,C17,C01
action: Where deadfall gathering is lawful, collect only dead and downed sticks already on the ground—never break branches off live or standing dead trees that provide wildlife habitat and fall hazards. @C17,E22,E13
action: Gather small-diameter downed pieces that burn cleanly to fine ash without sawing large logs or stripping the surrounding campsite bare. @C17,C04
failure: Hacking green branches off live conifers or transporting uncertified out-of-area firewood carrying invasive forest pests into state parks. @C17,C20,E22
verify: Confirm unit legality for deadfall or certified firewood, zero live-tree damage and modest fuel volume matched to a small fire. @C20,C17,ED
stop: If local rules prohibit wood gathering or open fires, leave all wood untouched and use an approved portable stove or no-fire plan. @C01,C13,C20
diagram: Verify unit wood rules | Use dead downed sticks only | Protect live trees & habitat
visual: Environmental forest-floor schematic distinguishing lawful downed deadwood collection from prohibited live-tree cutting and standing snag hazards. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Land management and forestry

Z03-P17 | Prepare fine shavings, fibrous tinder and kindling bundles safely | B012 P20 and Smokey Bear campfire guide; adapted historical material
objective: Create high-surface-area dry starter materials that catch brief ignition heat and hand flame reliably to kindling.
applies: Staging tinder and kindling for an authorized outdoor fire; requires safe cutting habits if using a knife.
equipment: Dry lawful tinder/wood, dry base surface, safe knife only if trained and extinguishing water nearby.
action: Match tinder fineness to your ignition tool—sparks require very fine dry fibrous material or prepared shavings, whereas open matches and lighters can ignite thin dry twigs and dry wood curls. @C03,C10,C11
action: Keep cutting strokes controlled away from fingers, legs and companions when making dry wood shavings from sound deadwood; use a stable base rather than holding wood against your body. @C03,T01,ED
action: Bundle fine tinder loosely enough for oxygen circulation and stage pencil-thin dry kindling right beside it so flame transfers smoothly before tinder burns out. @C03,ED
failure: Packing fine tinder tightly into a dense airless ball that smothers sparks, or carving hastily with cold numb hands and causing a laceration. @C03,E06,T01
verify: Confirm loose dry tinder, thin dry kindling and medium fuel are all staged on a dry surface before igniting. @C03,ED
stop: If cold numb fingers impair knife control or dry tinder cannot be prepared safely, put the blade away and switch to commercial dry tinder or no-fire shelter. @E06,C03,C08
diagram: Fine loose dry tinder | Thin dry transition kindling | Stable safe prep off ground
visual: Material cross-section comparing fine fibrous spark-receptive tinder, thin kindling shavings and dense wet wood that resists ignition. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and field tool safety

Z03-P18 | Stage fuel lay geometry for steady airflow without smothering | Smokey Bear guide and B012 P19; new manuscript
objective: Arrange kindling and fuel over tinder so rising heat preheats upper sticks while cool air feeds the base.
applies: Building a small authorized outdoor campfire in a cleared mineral-soil ring.
equipment: Staged dry tinder, progressive kindling sizes, cleared fire ring, water and stirring tool.
action: Place a dry wood or mineral base under tinder on damp ground and arrange fine kindling loosely above with an open windward air gap so flames climb cleanly upward. @C03,C02,ED
action: Leave space between sticks for oxygen draw—air feeds from underneath while heat rises through stacked kindling to dry and ignite the next size up. @C03,ED
action: Add medium fuel gradually only after kindling burns steadily, keeping all wood ends completely inside the fire ring perimeter. @C02,C03,C17
failure: Dropping heavy split logs straight onto a fragile match flame, crushing air channels and creating thick eye-stinging smoke without sustained heat. @C03,C02
verify: Confirm clear bottom airflow channel, gradual stick-size progression and all fuel contained strictly inside the cleared ring. @C02,C03,ED
stop: If gusts blow flames sideways out of the ring or fuel shifts unpredictably, stop adding wood and drown the fire completely. @C02,C04,C18
diagram: Cool air feeds underneath | Fine kindling catches flame | Gradual fuel stays in ring
visual: Cutaway fire-lay diagram showing bottom oxygen intake, rising convection column through graduated kindling and ring containment bounds. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and combustion safety

Z03-P19 | Keep fuel reserves dry off snow and outside the spark zone | B012 P19–P20 and Smokey Bear guide; adapted historical material
objective: Protect staged firewood from moisture while preventing stray sparks or radiant heat from igniting the reserve pile.
applies: Any authorized outdoor campfire in wet, snowy or windy settings.
equipment: Cleared ring, upwind/crosswind fuel staging spot, waterproof cover if needed and extinguishing water.
action: Stack spare fuel well clear of the fire ring on the upwind or crosswind side—never downwind where floating sparks and embers land directly on dry kindling. @C02,C18,ED
action: Elevate dry kindling and tinder reserves off wet snow or mud using a dry log or waterproof sheet so moisture does not wick upward into staged wood. @C03,ED
action: Keep portable stove fuel canisters, spare lighters, synthetic packs and shelter fabrics far away from open flame, radiant heat and popping embers. @C02,C15,C11
failure: Stacking spare dry branches right beside the downwind edge of a fire ring or resting a butane canister next to coals to warm it up. @C02,C15,C18
verify: Confirm fuel reserves stay dry, sit outside spark-fall distance and are physically separate from pressurized fuel bottles and camp gear. @C02,C15,ED
stop: Move reserves farther back or extinguish the fire immediately if sparks reach staged wood or wind shifts toward camp gear. @C02,C04,C18
diagram: Fire ring (downwind clear) | Upwind setback buffer | Dry fuel off snow; no canisters
visual: Environmental camp-ring layout separating the active fire ring, downwind ember corridor, upwind dry fuel stack and isolated gear/canisters. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and portable appliance safety

Z03-P20 | Reject liquid accelerants, aerosol cans and explosive fire starters | Smokey Bear guide and NPS fire safety; new manuscript
objective: Prevent flash burns, vapor explosions and uncontrollable wildfire ignition by never pouring liquid fuels on campfires.
applies: All outdoor campfires, grills and emergency fire-starting situations.
equipment: Approved dry tinder and commercial solid fire starters only; keep liquid stove fuels sealed and separate.
action: Never pour gasoline, camp stove white gas, kerosene, alcohol or lighter fluid onto open flames, warm coals or unlit wood—invisible vapors pool and flash explosively back to the container. @C03,C15,C02
action: Never toss empty or partial pressurized fuel canisters, aerosol cans or sealed containers into a campfire or hot ashes; trapped pressure ruptures violently with shrapnel and fireball risk. @C15,C02,ED
action: Use only dry natural tinder or purpose-made solid fire starters (such as wax-impregnated tabs) matched to your lawful site and ignition tool. @C03,C11
failure: Squirt-squeezing stove fuel onto smoldering wet logs to speed ignition, igniting fuel vapors around hands and clothing. @C03,C15
verify: Confirm all liquid stove fuels and pressurized canisters remain capped and stored well away from the fire ring. @C03,C15,ED
stop: If liquid fuel accidentally spills near a fire site, do NOT ignite anything there; evacuate the vapor zone and use a different safe site or no-fire plan. @C15,C02,C08
diagram: Liquid fuel vapors pool | Invisible flash burns back | Zero accelerants; solid dry tinder
visual: Failure-mode illustration showing invisible vapor flashback from liquid accelerants and pressurized-can rupture alongside safe solid-tinder practice. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and burn-injury safety

Z03-P21 | Compare disposable lighters, stormproof matches and ferrocerium rods | NPS Ten Essentials and B012 P19; new manuscript
objective: Select complementary modern ignition tools with different weather and mechanical failure modes.
applies: Packing fire-ignition kits for permitted trips and emergency preparedness packs.
equipment: Two independent ignition tools (e.g., butane lighter plus stormproof matches or ferrocerium rod) in waterproof storage.
action: Recognize that disposable butane lighters give fast easy flame in mild dry air, but lose vapor pressure in freezing cold and fail if the flint wheel gets wet. @C11,ED
action: Recognize that stormproof matches ignite reliably in wind and rain and burn briefly on their own, yet are single-use consumables limited by count and dry striker surfaces. @C11,ED
action: Recognize that ferrocerium rods work wet and in subfreezing air for thousands of strikes, yet produce only brief sparks that require well-prepared fine dry tinder and two-handed dexterity. @C11,C03,ED
failure: Packing a single disposable lighter for a winter hike and finding zero flame when cold hands and freezing air drop butane pressure. @C11,E06
verify: Confirm your kit carries at least two ignition tools with independent failure modes, stored dry and tested safely before departure. @C11,E02,ED
stop: Do not rely on untested friction skills as your primary field ignition method; always carry reliable modern ignition plus a no-fire insulation plan. @C11,C08,C12
diagram: Lighter: fast, cold-sensitive | Storm matches: windproof, finite | Ferro rod: wet-proof, needs fine tinder
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting reliability, cold/wet tolerance and tinder demands across butane lighters, stormproof matches and ferrocerium rods. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Field equipment and fire prevention

Z03-P22 | Manage butane lighter cold-vapor failure and wet striker wheels | NPS Ten Essentials and cold-weather field safety; new manuscript
objective: Keep disposable or piezo lighters functional in cold or damp weather and diagnose common mechanical failures quickly.
applies: Carrying and operating standard butane lighters in cool, wet or winter outdoor conditions.
equipment: Standard butane lighter stored in an inner pocket, dry spare lighter and waterproof backup matches or ferro rod.
action: Carry a butane lighter in an interior pocket close to body warmth during freezing weather so liquid butane stays warm enough to vaporize when the valve opens. @C11,E10,ED
action: Protect flint-wheel lighters in waterproof bags; if a flint wheel gets wet, dry it thoroughly by rolling it gently without depressing the gas lever before trying to light. @C11,ED
action: Recognize that piezo-electric igniters can misfire at high elevation or in damp air, and never warm a cold lighter near open flame or hot stoves—pressurized fuel canisters can burst. @C11,C15,ED
failure: Keeping a plastic butane lighter in an outer pack pocket at 10°F, then repeatedly holding the gas open until fuel freezes or runs empty without lighting. @C11,ED
verify: Test the lighter safely outdoors away from flammable vapor before trip departure and verify your independent non-butane backup works. @C11,E02,ED
stop: If a lighter leaks gas, cracks or fails to light cleanly after warming in your hand, retire it and switch to stormproof matches, ferro rod or the no-fire plan. @C11,C08,ED
diagram: Cold drops butane vapor | Wet flint wheel skips sparks | Warm in pocket & carry backup
visual: Failure-mode diagram linking cold vapor-pressure drop and wet striker wheels to failed flame cues and pocket-warming or backup-tool recovery. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Field equipment and fire prevention

Z03-P23 | Use stormproof and waterproof matches reliably in wind and damp air | NPS Ten Essentials and Smokey Bear guide; new manuscript
objective: Protect match heads and striker strips from moisture and shield brief flames cleanly on authorized sites.
applies: Igniting staged dry tinder in breezy or damp conditions where open fires or stoves are permitted.
equipment: Waterproof case with stormproof matches, dry spare striker strip and pre-staged tinder/kindling.
action: Store stormproof matches and their dedicated striker strips inside a gasketed waterproof container—even waterproof matches fail if the striker surface dissolves in water. @C11,ED
action: Stage tinder and kindling completely first, turn your body to block gusts and strike firmly away from your body and away from the open match container. @C03,C11,C02
action: Hold the burning match steadily beneath the fine dry tinder base until flame transfers, then dispose of the cooled spent match only inside the cleared fire ring or pack out cold. @C03,C04,ED
failure: Opening a match case with wet hands, ruining the sole striker strip, or lighting matches one by one before kindling is staged. @C11,C03
verify: Confirm the container gasket seals tightly, a dry backup striker sits inside and tinder is fully staged before striking. @C11,C03,ED
stop: Stop burning through remaining emergency matches if tinder is too wet to catch; dry/prepare better tinder or shift to the no-fire warmth plan. @C03,C08,C11
diagram: Gasketed dry case + striker | Stage dry tinder first | Shield wind & transfer cleanly
visual: Exploded kit view showing waterproof match case, sealing O-ring, protected striker strip and wind-shielded ignition transfer. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Field equipment and fire prevention

Z03-P24 | Understand ferrocerium rod scraping mechanics and spark direction | B008/B010 ignition plates and NPS Ten Essentials; adapted historical material
objective: Understand how a sharp striker shaves burning pyrophoric ferrocerium particles cleanly into prepared fine tinder.
applies: Supervised practice or lawful outdoor ignition using a commercial ferrocerium rod and dedicated striker.
equipment: Ferrocerium rod, sharp 90-degree striker (not a knife's sharp cutting edge), receptive fine dry tinder and fire-control water.
action: Brace the tip of the ferrocerium rod firmly on a stable surface right beside the dry tinder bundle—stabilizing the rod prevents knocking the tinder apart mid-strike. @C11,C03,ED
action: Scrape firmly along the rod with a sharp square-spined striker (or pull the rod back cleanly against a stationary striker) to shave hot oxidizing ferrocerium particles directly onto fine tinder. @C11,C03,ED
action: Avoid striking with a knife's cutting edge, which dulls the blade and risks severe hand lacerations if the blade slips toward fingers or legs. @T01,ED
failure: Waving a ferro rod wildly in midair above coarse sticks, scattering sparks harmlessly away from the tinder or slicing a hand with a knife blade. @C03,T01,ED
verify: In a cold dry-run position check, confirm stable braced tip placement, sharp square striker spine and fine fibrous tinder directly in the spark path. @C03,C11,ED
stop: Keep live sparks strictly on authorized cleared sites with water ready; if cold numb hands prevent controlled scraping, use a lighter, matches or no-fire warmth. @C01,C02,C08,E06
diagram: Brace rod tip beside tinder | Square striker shaves metal | Hot sparks land in fine tinder
visual: Mechanism diagram illustrating braced rod geometry, 90-degree striker shear angle and pyrophoric particle arc into fibrous tinder. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and materials mechanics

Z03-P25 | Distinguish traditional steel-and-flint sparks from ferrocerium sparks | B008/B010 and NPS Pipe Spring flint-and-steel; adapted historical material
objective: Contrast the physics, spark temperature and tinder requirements of traditional flint-and-steel versus modern ferrocerium rods.
applies: Historical interpretation, mechanism education and supervised firecraft training.
equipment: Identified traditional carbon-steel striker and flint/chert edge alongside a modern ferrocerium rod for cold comparison.
action: Recognize that traditional flint-and-steel sheds tiny particles of carbon STEEL shaved off by a hard rock edge (flint/chert), producing cooler short-lived sparks that require charred cloth or specialized punkwood to catch an ember. @C10
action: Recognize that a modern ferrocerium rod sheds engineered pyrophoric alloy shavings that burn far hotter and longer, able to ignite fine dry fibrous tinder or commercial tabs directly. @C10,C11,ED
action: Keep historical flint-and-steel clearly classified as a skill-intensive educational or supervised heritage technique rather than substituting it for dependable modern emergency ignition. @C10,C11,C08
failure: Assuming any random shiny rock from a creek bed sparks like a ferrocerium rod or expecting traditional steel sparks to light ordinary twigs directly. @C10,C03
verify: Explain clearly which material sheds the spark in each system and why charred tinder is required for traditional steel sparks. @C10,ED
stop: Conduct first comparisons cold without live flame; require site authorization, eye caution against rock chips and ready water for any supervised live strike. @C01,C02,C10
diagram: Flint-and-steel: steel sheds sparks | Needs charred tinder -> ember | Ferro rod: hotter alloy sparks
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting spark origin, particle energy and required tinder receptivity between traditional flint-and-steel and ferrocerium rods. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Materials mechanics and fire prevention

Z03-P26 | Understand charred-material spark reception and ember transfer | NPS Pipe Spring flint-and-steel and B008 mechanisms; adapted historical material
objective: Explain how charred cellulose catches low-energy sparks and transitions a glowing coal into a flame-ready tinder bundle.
applies: Conceptual firecraft education and supervised training on authorized cleared sites; NOT a field-validated cold-weather emergency shortcut.
equipment: Prepared dry charred cloth or identified dry punkwood, loose dry fibrous tinder nest, cleared ring and water.
action: Understand that charring drives off volatile moisture and leaves porous carbonized fibers with a lower ignition threshold able to glow from brief steel sparks. @C10,C03,ED
action: Trace the two-stage transition: a spark starts a slow glowing ember on charred material, and gentle steady airflow inside a loose dry fibrous bundle raises heat until open flame appears. @C10,C03,ED
action: Keep charred materials sealed completely dry and cool before storage—improperly extinguished char tins or smoldering cloth can reignite inside a pack or pocket. @C04,C11,ED
failure: Blowing violently on a fragile ember until it flies out of the bundle, or pocketing warm charred cloth that smolders through pack fabric. @C03,C04,ED
verify: Trace the progression spark -> glowing ember -> aerated dry bundle -> flame conceptually before any supervised live practice. @C10,C03,ED
stop: Never inhale thick tinder-bundle smoke, never practice indoors and drown all glowing embers completely cold when training ends. @C04,C05,C01
diagram: Spark catches porous char | Ember glows in loose fibers | Gentle airflow -> open flame
visual: Material cross-section showing porous charred carbon fibers transferring glowing heat into an aerated dry fibrous tinder nest. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and materials mechanics

Z03-P27 | Trace friction-ember dust formation and notch geometry in wood drills | B008/B010 historical plates and NPS Forces and Tools; adapted historical material
objective: Explain the mechanical role of wood dust accumulation, hearth notch shape and downward pressure in friction fire sets.
applies: Conceptual mechanism study and supervised training; preserves historical B008/B010 mechanism plates separately without claiming field-validated emergency success.
equipment: Identified training bow-drill or hand-drill components inspected cold (hearth board, spindle, notch, catch leaf, bearing block).
action: Trace how downward force plus reciprocating rotation grinds fine dry wood dust at the spindle-hearth socket and heats that dust through mechanical friction. @C12
action: Identify the V-shaped hearth notch as the dust collection channel: it lets heated dark-brown wood powder spill into one compact pile where insulated heat can self-sustain as a glowing ember. @C12,ED
action: Recognize that visible white smoke during drilling only signals warming wood—not yet a viable ember; stopping too early or disturbing the fragile dust pile collapses ignition. @C12,ED
failure: Expecting an un-notched hearth hole to produce an ember, or spending hours grinding wet unknown woods while hypothermia sets in. @C12,C08,E06
verify: Point to the bearing block, rotating spindle, hearth socket, V-notch dust pile and catch surface on a cold unlit assembly. @C12,ED
stop: Treat friction drilling as a supervised training skill, not a primary cold-weather survival guarantee; abort immediately if effort causes sweating, exhaustion or worsening exposure. @C12,C08,E10
diagram: Downward pressure + spin | V-notch collects hot dust | Compact pile sustains ember
visual: Exploded component view of a wood-friction hearth and spindle labeling socket friction zone, V-notch dust channel and catch surface. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Mechanism reviewer and fire prevention

Z03-P28 | Compare canister, liquid-fuel and solid-fuel stoves by temperature and altitude | NPS wildland fire safety and USFS fire restrictions; new manuscript
objective: Match portable outdoor stove types to ambient temperature, wind, elevation and local fire-restriction rules.
applies: Selecting a camp stove for boiling water and cooking across three-season, winter and fire-restricted backcountry trips.
equipment: Manufacturer stove specifications, matched fuel and current land-unit fire restriction order.
action: Recognize that upright isobutane/propane canisters are simple and have an on/off shutoff valve (often permitted under Stage 1/2 fire restrictions where open fires are banned), yet lose pressure sharply in freezing cold unless inverted/regulated per manufacturer design. @C13,C15,ED
action: Recognize that pumped liquid-fuel stoves maintain strong output in subfreezing temperatures and high elevation, yet require careful outdoor priming, leak checks and manufacturer-matched fuel. @C15,ED
action: Recognize that solid-fuel tablet or alcohol stoves lack an instant shutoff valve, are frequently prohibited during Stage 1/2 fire restrictions and burn weakly in wind without careful shielding. @C13,C15,ED
failure: Taking an unregulated canister stove into 5°F winter air without testing cold output, or using an open alcohol stove during a Stage 2 shutoff-valve-only restriction. @C13,C15
verify: Confirm the stove matches local fire restriction rules (on/off valve requirement), forecast low temperature and manufacturer-approved fuel type. @C13,C15,ED
stop: Never operate any stove indoors or in a tent vestibule, and never substitute unapproved fuels in a stove rated for a specific fuel. @C05,C15,C16
diagram: Canister: valve, cold-limited | Liquid: cold-strong, prime carefully | Solid/alcohol: often banned in stages
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting shutoff-valve restriction compliance, cold-weather performance and handling hazards across three stove classes. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Portable appliance safety and fire prevention

Z03-P29 | Refuel liquid stoves safely on cleared ground away from ignition | NPS wildland fire safety and USFS campfire guidance; new manuscript
objective: Prevent fuel-vapor flash fires and spilled-fuel flare-ups when filling or priming liquid-fuel camp stoves.
applies: Operating white-gas, kerosene or multi-fuel liquid camp stoves outdoors.
equipment: Manufacturer-approved fuel bottle and pour spout, cooled stove, level cleared mineral-soil surface and dry rag.
action: Extinguish the stove completely and wait until the burner, windscreen and fuel tank feel cool before cracking open any fuel cap or pump assembly. @C15
action: Refuel strictly outdoors on level bare ground well away from tents, campfires, lit stoves, smoking materials and food-preparation areas. @C15,C02
action: Wipe away any fuel drips, cap the supply bottle tightly and move the stove several steps away from any spilled ground fuel before attempting to light it. @C15,ED
failure: Cracking a warm stove pump open beside a companion's burning stove or lighting a fuel-soaked stove right over a fresh white-gas spill. @C15
verify: Confirm stove body is cool, nearby ignition sources are out, fuel bottle is sealed and stove stands on clean dry ground away from drips. @C15,ED
stop: If fuel leaks from pump seals, hose threads or tank seams during pressurization, depressurize outdoors away from ignition and retire the stove from service. @C15,C05
diagram: Cool stove completely | Refuel outdoors clear of flames | Move away from any spill
visual: Exploded refueling-safety sequence showing cooled tank, sealed supply bottle, spill separation zone and cleared outdoor lighting pad. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Portable appliance safety and fire prevention

Z03-P30 | Match stove windscreens, pot supports and fuel bottles to manufacturer specs | NPS fire safety and CPSC camping equipment; new manuscript
objective: Prevent fuel-canister overheating, burst pressure valves and pot tip-overs when using windscreens and accessories.
applies: Setting up canister or liquid camp stoves in breezy outdoor conditions.
equipment: Stove, manufacturer-approved windscreen (if permitted for that stove), level base and matched fuel canister or bottle.
action: Never wrap a tight full-enclosure foil windscreen around a canister-top stove—trapped radiant heat can overheat the pressurized fuel canister underneath and cause a catastrophic rupture. @C15,ED
action: Follow the exact manufacturer instructions for windscreen clearance and remote-canister routing; use a natural rock or pack windblock set well back instead of trapping heat around canisters. @C15,ED
action: Match pot diameter to stove arm limits and place the stove on a flat non-flammable surface so boiling pots cannot tip onto operators or dry needles. @C15,C02
failure: Wrapping heavy aluminum foil snugly around a threaded isobutane canister until the metal becomes too hot to touch. @C15
verify: Confirm fuel canister stays cool to touch during operation, pot sits centered on stable supports and surrounding ground is cleared of dry needles. @C15,C02,ED
stop: Shut off the stove immediately if the fuel canister grows noticeably warm, flames lick around the tank or pot supports wobble. @C15,C05
diagram: Canister stays cool & vented | Matched windscreen clearance | Stable level pot support
visual: Exploded stove-accessory layout showing safe open canister ventilation versus prohibited tight foil wrap overheating hazard. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Portable appliance safety

Z03-P31 | Interpret Stage 1, Stage 2 and Red Flag fire restrictions accurately | USFS fire restrictions, MN DNR and NWS wildfire; new manuscript
objective: Translate official fire-danger ratings, restriction stages and Red Flag Warnings into immediate legal compliance.
applies: All public lands, national forests, state parks and rural camps during dry, windy or fire-season periods.
equipment: Current agency fire-restriction order, NOAA Weather Radio or alert check and compliant stove or no-fire meal plan.
action: Check current restrictions for the exact county, state forest, park or national forest unit immediately before travel—rules change daily with humidity and drought. @C01,C13
action: Recognize the typical restriction progression: Stage 1 generally limits campfires to developed metal rings and bans dispersed wood fires; Stage 2 generally bans all wood and charcoal fires while sometimes allowing liquid/gas stoves with an on/off valve in cleared areas. @C13,C01
action: Treat a Red Flag Warning (low humidity plus high wind) as an automatic stop to open flames—even where campfires are technically legal, sparks escape containment rapidly. @C18,C02
failure: Assuming a fire ring at a backcountry campsite means fires are currently allowed during a regional burn ban or Red Flag Warning. @C01,C13,C18
verify: Record the current local restriction level, effective date and whether your specific stove type is explicitly permitted before lighting. @C01,C13,ED
stop: When local fire orders ban open flames or stoves, or when current status cannot be verified, use cold meals, battery lights and insulated layers exclusively. @C01,C13,C08
diagram: Check local unit order | Stage 1/2 limits open wood | Red Flag = automatic no-fire
visual: Decision tree routing local burn status, Stage 1/2 orders and Red Flag Warnings to lawful fire, shutoff-stove-only or strict no-fire compliance. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Wildland fire prevention and land management

Z03-P32 | Follow certified local firewood rules and designated fire-ring limits | Minnesota DNR park rules and NPS Leave No Trace; new manuscript
objective: Prevent invasive forest-pest introductions and illegal fire scars when camping in parks and forests.
applies: State parks, campgrounds and managed recreation areas across Minnesota and the Upper Midwest.
equipment: Local park fire regulations, approved/certified local firewood and established designated fire ring.
action: Buy or obtain only local or state-certified firewood approved by the park unit; transporting untreated firewood across counties spreads emerald ash borer and other destructive forest pests. @C20
action: Build fires strictly inside designated metal or stone fire rings where required—do not stack new rock rings on untouched soil or burn trash, plastics or foil inside rings. @C20,C02,C17
action: Burn firewood completely down to cold ash and pack out unburned food wrappers or foil after verifying full extinguishment. @C04,C17,C20
failure: Hauling home yard-tree logs across regions into a state park or leaving half-burned trash smoldering in a makeshift rock circle. @C20,C04,C17
verify: Confirm firewood meets current unit certification rules, fire stays inside the designated ring and zero trash enters the fire. @C20,C02,ED
stop: If approved firewood is unavailable or designated rings are closed, skip open fires and use a permitted stove or no-fire food and warmth options. @C20,C01,C08
diagram: Certified local firewood | Designated ring only | Burn clean; pack out trash
visual: Decision tree verifying park firewood certification, designated-ring legality and Leave No Trace clean-burn requirements. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Forestry, land management and fire prevention

Z03-P33 | Recognize wind-driven ember transport and dry-fuel escape hazards | NWS wildfire weather and Smokey Bear guide; new manuscript
objective: Spot wind gusts, dry fine fuels and convective ember lofting before a small campfire escapes into wildland vegetation.
applies: Any outdoor fire site surrounded by dry grass, pine needles, brush or forested ridges.
equipment: Weather forecast, extinguishing water, shovel/stirring tool and live wind observation.
action: Watch treetops, smoke angle and rising sparks—steady breezes or sudden gusts can carry glowing embers well beyond a cleared mineral ring into dry grass or needle beds downwind. @C18,C02
action: Recognize that fine dead fuels (cured grass, dry pine needles, birch bark curls) ignite within seconds during low relative humidity and spread faster uphill with wind alignment. @C18,C02,C14
action: Keep water and a stirring tool staged outside the smoke path and cancel or drown any campfire the moment gusts lift sparks outside the ring perimeter. @C02,C04,C18
failure: Leaving a crackling pine-wood fire unattended for five minutes while an afternoon breeze lifts glowing bark embers into dry grass ten yards downwind. @C02,C04,C18
verify: Confirm calm controllable wind, zero escaping sparks and full downwind fuel scan before adding another stick. @C02,C18,ED
stop: Any escaping spark into surrounding brush, rising gusts or active Red Flag conditions requires immediate fire extinguishment; if fire spreads beyond instant control, evacuate and call 911. @C04,C18,C19,T02
diagram: Gust lofts glowing embers | Dry fine fuels ignite downwind | Drown fire or evacuate early
visual: Environmental wind-transport schematic showing ember lofting from a campfire ring across dry fine fuels and uphill spread danger. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Wildland fire behavior and prevention

Z03-P34 | Detect underground root, duff and peat fires hidden beneath ash or snow | USFS campfire safety and Smokey Bear guide; new manuscript
objective: Prevent holdover wildfires that smolder unseen underground for hours or days after surface flames disappear.
applies: Forested sites, boreal/peat soils, winter snow-covered forest floors and any extinguished campfire check.
equipment: Abundant extinguishing water, sturdy stirring tool and bare-hand radiant heat check (without touching hot embers).
action: Recognize that tree roots, deep pine duff and peat bogs hold oxygen and dry organic fuel below the surface—a fire built over duff or snow can burn downward unseen even after top ash looks gray. @C14,C04
action: Never bury a campfire solely with dirt or snow to put it out; burying insulates hot coals and roots so they continue smoldering underground and re-emerge in later wind. @C04,C14
action: Drown thoroughly with water, stir deep into the wet ash slurry, probe ring edges for buried steaming roots and repeat until every spot feels completely cool without wisps of steam or smoke. @C04,C14
failure: Shoveling dry sand or snow over glowing coals at checkout, leaving a buried root fire that flares into a wildfire two days later. @C04,C14
verify: Pass the back of your hand close above the stirred wet ash and perimeter soil—confirm zero warmth, hissing or smoke anywhere. @C04,ED
stop: If smoking soil or burning roots persist beneath a fire site despite watering, keep dousing if safe or report an active ground fire immediately to land managers or 911. @C04,C14,T02
diagram: Surface ash looks gray | Buried duff & roots smolder | Drown, stir deep & feel cold
visual: Cutaway soil profile exposing smoldering subsurface tree roots and peat duff beneath gray surface ash and ineffective dirt burial. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Wildland fire prevention and forestry

Z03-P35 | Recognize why snow platforms melt and destabilize winter fires | B012 P19 winter fire and Smokey Bear guide; adapted historical material
objective: Understand how winter snowpack melts beneath heat, soaking fires and exposing hidden dry forest duff underneath.
applies: Authorized winter campfires built where deep snow covers the forest floor.
equipment: Lawful fire authorization, heat-safe stove alternative if snow is deep, extinguishing water and stirring tool.
action: Recognize that snow is NOT a reliable fireproof floor—heat rapidly melts a deep pit straight down through snowpack, dumping water onto embers while collapsing the fuel stack sideways. @C02,C03,ED
action: Recognize the opposite winter trap: once a fire melts down to ground level beneath snow, dry protected forest duff and roots underneath can ignite out of sight beneath overhanging snow shelves. @C14,C04,ED
action: Prefer a rated outdoor winter stove on a stable insulated base (such as a stove board per manufacturer guidance) over trying to maintain an open wood fire in deep winter snow. @C15,C17,ED
failure: Lighting a small emergency fire directly on soft winter snow, watching it sink two feet into wet slush and exhaust the group's dry tinder. @C03,C08,ED
verify: Confirm winter heat/boiling relies on a stable outdoor stove or an authorized cleared mineral-soil ring with full cold-ash verification. @C15,C14,C04,ED
stop: If melting snow collapses the fire bed or steam/smoke escapes beneath snowbanks, drown the remnants cold and shift to stove or no-fire insulation. @C04,C08,C15
diagram: Heat melts snowpit beneath | Fuel collapses & wets out | Hidden duff may ignite below
visual: Failure-mode illustration showing snowpack melt-collapse extinguishing wood fires while risking concealed sub-snow duff ignition. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather operations and fire prevention

Z03-P36 | Diagnose smoky non-sustaining ignition attempts and damp fuel failures | B012 P20 and Smokey Bear campfire guide; adapted historical material
objective: Identify why an ignition attempt produces thick smoke or brief flashes without sustaining a flame, and decide when to stop.
applies: Authorized outdoor campfire lighting in damp, humid or windy weather.
equipment: Dry prepared tinder, pencil-thin dry kindling and an explicit time/energy cutoff for switching to no-fire warmth.
action: Match the failure cue to its cause: heavy white smoke with no flame signals damp tinder or blocked airflow; a bright brief flash that dies immediately signals missing fine dry transition kindling. @C03,ED
action: Pause and fix fuel preparation BEFORE striking more matches—split dry inner twigs, elevate the base off damp ground and open bottom airflow rather than feeding more wet sticks. @C03,C11,ED
action: Set a hard time and energy cutoff in cold weather; repeated failed fire attempts waste precious calories, chill bare fingers and delay shelter setup. @C08,E06,ED
failure: Burning through an entire box of matches under wet leafy twigs while hands go numb and wind cools the whole group. @C03,C08,E06
verify: Confirm dry interior tinder, graduated pencil-thin kindling and clear airflow are corrected before attempting another strike. @C03,ED
stop: If two disciplined attempts fail because fuel is soaked or wind is too strong, stop trying to build a fire and commit immediately to dry insulation and shelter. @C08,E05,C03
diagram: Thick smoke or brief flash | Wet fuel or missing kindling | Fix prep once or switch to shelter
visual: Failure-mode illustration mapping white smoke and flash-out symptoms to wet fuel or missing transition kindling, with a cold-weather abort cutoff. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and cold-weather operations

Z03-P37 | Diagnose friction-drill polishing, cord slip and resin glazing in training | B008/B010 mechanism plates and NPS Forces and Tools; adapted historical material
objective: Recognize common mechanical failure modes during supervised wood-friction firecraft practice.
applies: Supervised educational practice with identified training wood sets on authorized sites; conceptual troubleshooting only.
equipment: Cold training bow-drill assembly inspected with an instructor.
action: Recognize shiny glazed black wood inside the hearth socket (polishing) as a sign of resinous wood, mismatched wood hardness or excess pressure without fine dust generation—dust must look dry, dark brown and powdery. @C12,ED
action: Recognize squeaking spindles or a slipping bowstring as insufficient cord tension, glossy spindle surface or excessive friction at the top bearing block rather than the bottom hearth contact. @C12,ED
action: Diagnose and adjust components cold between controlled training runs rather than exhausting learners in continuous fruitless sawing. @C12,E10,ED
failure: Confusing shiny burned squeaking wood and white steam with near-ember success during a training session. @C12,ED
verify: Identify the difference between coarse light shavings, glazed polished sockets and fine dark-brown ember-capable powder on a cold training set. @C12,ED
stop: End live friction trials immediately if materials are damp/resinous, hands blister or fatigue impairs safety; never rely on unpracticed friction drilling in an actual cold emergency. @C12,C08,E10
diagram: Glazed socket or slipping cord | Zero fine dark powder forms | Adjust cold or switch method
visual: Failure-mode illustration diagnosing socket glazing, cord slip and bearing drag in friction drills alongside training-only boundaries. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Mechanism reviewer and fire prevention

Z03-P38 | Recognize fuel leaks, flare-ups and clogged jets on portable stoves | NPS wildland fire safety and CPSC camping equipment; new manuscript
objective: Spot dangerous stove malfunctions early and execute a calm outdoor shutdown without causing burns or fires.
applies: Operating canister or liquid-fuel outdoor camp stoves.
equipment: Stove manufacturer manual, cleared outdoor site, safe shutoff access and non-stove food/water backup.
action: Watch and smell for liquid fuel seepage at bottle threads, hissing unlit canister valves, uneven yellow sputtering flames or sudden priming flare-ups before placing a pot on the burner. @C15,C05
action: If a stove flares high, keep face and hands back, turn off the fuel control valve ONLY if safely reachable without crossing flame—otherwise step back on clear ground and let contained residual fuel burn out safely while protecting nearby gear. @C15,T01,ED
action: Never pour water directly onto a burning liquid-fuel pool (water spreads floating gasoline/white-gas flames); keep stoves strictly on cleared non-combustible surfaces outdoors. @C15,C02,ED
failure: Grabbing a flaring liquid-fuel stove inside a vestibule to toss it outside, splashing burning fuel across clothes and tent nylon. @C05,C15,C16
verify: Confirm tight leak-free fuel connections, steady blue controlled flame and unobstructed shutoff reach before cooking. @C15,ED
stop: Retire any leaking, damaged or persistently flaring stove immediately and switch to cold food, alternate water treatment or emergency assistance. @C15,C05,W09
diagram: Fuel hiss, leak or yellow flare | Never pour water on liquid fuel | Shut off safely or step back
visual: Failure-mode analysis showing fuel-thread leaks and priming flare-ups with safe valve shutoff, cleared-ground burn-out and zero-indoor-use boundaries. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Portable appliance safety and fire prevention

Z03-P39 | Respond to an escaped campfire or approaching wildfire early | Ready.gov wildfires, NWS wildfire and ARC first aid; new manuscript
objective: Separate instant suppression of a tiny spark from immediate life-safety evacuation when fire escapes or wildfire approaches.
applies: Campgrounds, backcountry trails and rural areas during dry seasons or active wildfire incidents.
equipment: Ready water and tool for tiny immediate sparks, alert receiver, mapped open exits and emergency contact access.
action: If a tiny stray ember ignites dry grass right beside the ring and is immediately safe to douse with staged water, drown and scrape it out at once—never delay calling help if flames grow or move into brush. @C04,C02,T01
action: If a fire escapes instant control or an official wildfire evacuation warning arrives, abandon camp equipment immediately and evacuate upwind or crosswind along an open verified road or exit—do not stay to fight advancing wildland fire. @C19,T13,T02
action: Call 911 as soon as safely clear to report exact location, wind direction, fuel type and any trapped people, and protect airways from heavy smoke indoors or per official guidance. @T02,T06,C19
failure: Spending minutes trying to stomp out wind-driven grass flames with boots while smoke cuts off the sole trailhead road. @C19,T13,T01
verify: Confirm all group members know the immediate evacuation route, rally point and 911 reporting details before any fire is lit. @C19,T13,T06,ED
stop: Life safety always overrides camp gear and fire fighting—never enter active flame corridors, dense blinding smoke or closed evacuation zones. @C19,T13,T01
diagram: Tiny ring spark -> douse now | Escaped flame -> leave gear & exit | Call 911 from safe clear ground
visual: Decision tree separating immediate single-spark suppression from mandatory camp abandonment, upwind/crosswind evacuation and 911 activation. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Wildland fire safety and emergency management

Z03-P40 | Enforce zero indoor charcoal, stove or unvented heater rules | CPSC camping equipment and CDC CO basics; new manuscript
objective: Prevent fatal carbon monoxide poisoning during power outages, winter storms and cold camping trips.
applies: Homes, basements, garages, enclosed porches, cabins, campers, tents and parked vehicles.
equipment: Battery-backed CO alarms, safe outdoor cooking space, insulated clothing/blankets and utility outage plan.
action: Never burn charcoal grills, hibachis, camp stoves, kerosene/propane camping heaters or wood braziers inside a house, basement, tent, camper, vehicle or garage—even with doors or windows open. @C05,C16
action: Recognize that glowing charcoal emits massive invisible, odorless carbon monoxide long after flames vanish, making indoor grilling or warming rapidly lethal. @C16,C07
action: Place portable fuel generators strictly outdoors at least 20 feet from any building with exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, and verify working CO alarms on every level. @C06,C09
failure: Moving a warm charcoal grill or camp stove into a garage or tent doorway out of the rain, filling the attached living space with lethal CO. @C05,C16,C07
verify: Confirm zero fuel-burning outdoor gear operates inside any structure or garage and that household CO alarms pass test checks. @C16,C06,C09,ED
stop: If a CO alarm sounds or anyone develops headache, dizziness, nausea or confusion, evacuate immediately to fresh outdoor air and call 911. @C07,C09,T02
diagram: Charcoal/stoves/gens OUTDOORS | Zero indoor or garage operation | Alarm or symptoms -> fresh air 911
visual: Cutaway building and camp boundary marking absolute prohibition of charcoal, camp stoves and heaters indoors or in open-door garages. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Carbon monoxide safety and emergency medicine

Z03-P41 | Recognize invisible carbon monoxide buildup at altitude and in partial enclosures | CPSC portable camping equipment and CDC CO basics; new manuscript
objective: Understand why cold air, high elevation and semi-enclosed spaces accelerate silent CO accumulation.
applies: High-elevation camps, wind-sheltered porches, carports, snow trenches and cold-weather stove use.
equipment: Open-air outdoor cooking location, battery lighting and symptom awareness across all group members.
action: Recognize that carbon monoxide is completely colorless, odorless and tasteless—you cannot detect dangerous buildup by smell, smoke visibility or clean blue flame color. @C07,C16
action: Recognize that high elevation lowers available oxygen and cold cooking pots quench flames, increasing incomplete combustion and CO output from camping stoves and heaters. @C05,C16
action: Treat semi-enclosed carports, screened porches, snow-walled wind pits and open-door garages as trapped-exhaust enclosures where wind eddies recirculate lethal CO back toward people. @C16,C06
failure: Assuming a clean blue propane flame inside an open-sided snow pit or partly open garage produces no dangerous carbon monoxide. @C05,C16,C07
verify: Confirm all fuel combustion occurs in fully open outdoor air with exhaust carried cleanly away from occupied areas and air intakes. @C05,C06,C16,ED
stop: Any flu-like headache, nausea, fatigue or confusion near combustion equipment demands immediate escape to open fresh air and emergency medical evaluation. @C07,T02
diagram: Cold/altitude -> higher CO | Semi-enclosure traps exhaust | Odorless hazard -> open air only
visual: Mechanism diagram showing incomplete combustion at altitude/cold pots combined with wind-eddy CO recirculation inside partial enclosures. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Carbon monoxide safety and combustion engineering

Z03-P42 | Use insulated layers, warm dry food and chemical handwarmers without fire | NWS winter weather, NPS Ten Essentials and CDC hypothermia; new manuscript
objective: Maintain core body heat and extremity function reliably when campfires are banned, impossible or unsafe.
applies: Cold travel, burn bans, wet forests, high-wind exposure and indoor power outages without safe vented heat.
equipment: Dry wool/synthetic layers, insulated sleeping system, ground pad, high-calorie no-cook food and optional sealed air-activated handwarmers.
action: Reduce heat loss first by getting out of wind, insulating underneath from cold ground and changing into dry wool or synthetic layers with a hat and mittens. @C08,E05,E10
action: Support internal metabolic heat with easy-to-eat high-calorie foods and safe hydration while pacing light gentle movement without working up a chill-inducing sweat. @E10,E05,W09,ED
action: If using commercial air-activated chemical warmers, follow package directions and keep a cloth layer between warmers and bare or numb skin to prevent contact burns. @E05,T04,ED
failure: Trying desperately to build an illegal or wet wood fire for two hours instead of eating calories, putting on dry spare insulation and climbing onto a foam pad. @C08,E05,E10
verify: Confirm wind block, dry underbody pad, dry head/hand/core layers and no-cook calories are active without needing combustion. @C08,E05,ED
stop: If shivering worsens into fumbling, slurred speech, confusion or severe drowsiness, treat as medical hypothermia and call 911 immediately. @E06,T03,T02
diagram: Dry layers + ground pad | High-calorie no-cook fuel | Barriered warmers; zero flame
visual: Comparative configuration showing three non-combustion warmth pillars (insulation/wind block, caloric fueling, protected chemical warmers). Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Cold-weather shelter and wilderness medicine

Z03-P43 | Use non-combustion signals and lighting when fires are banned or unsafe | NPS Ten Essentials and NPS outdoor emergency plan; new manuscript
objective: Signal rescuers and illuminate camp tasks effectively without risking wildfires or wasting energy on signal fires.
applies: Lost-person waiting, night emergencies, burn bans and wooded or windy terrain where fires are unsafe.
equipment: Pea-less whistle, LED headlamp with spare batteries, signal mirror, high-contrast ground cloth and phone/satellite communicator.
action: Use an LED headlamp or flashlight with spare power for night work and periodic visual signaling—keeping light independent of fuel or fire permission. @C11,E02
action: Use a loud pea-less whistle (three sharp blasts as a widely recognized distress call) and bright contrasting fabric or mirror flashes in open clearings where searchers or aircraft can spot them. @T06,E02,ED
action: Rely on phone/satellite location reports and non-combustion signals whenever burn bans, wind or dry fuels make an open signal fire illegal or dangerous to the stranded party. @C01,C18,T06
failure: Lighting a smoky brush fire in a dry forest to signal searchers, trapping the stranded group in a fast-moving wildfire. @C01,C18,C19
verify: Confirm whistle, working headlamp, spare batteries and bright visibility panel are immediately accessible in the top pack pocket. @E02,C11,ED
stop: Never ignite a wildland fire as a signal; prioritize safe open visibility, audio/LED signaling and verified electronic contact when service permits. @C01,C18,T06
diagram: LED light + spare batteries | Whistle & high-vis fabric | Safe electronic/visual signaling
visual: Comparative configuration contrasting reliable non-combustion audio, visual-contrast and LED/electronic signaling options against hazardous signal fires. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Search and rescue and fire prevention

Z03-P44 | Set up a supervised controlled practice site for ignition training | Smokey Bear guide, USFS fire safety and B008/B010; new manuscript
objective: Practice ignition mechanics safely under controlled non-emergency conditions with zero uncontrolled spread risk.
applies: Educational workshops, personal skill rehearsal and evaluating fire-starting gear prior to field reliance.
equipment: Lawfully authorized cleared mineral-soil pit or metal pan, abundant water bucket, stirring tool, first-aid/burn awareness and supervisor.
action: Schedule skills practice only on low fire-danger days with current legal authorization, calm wind and a cleared mineral-soil ring or fire-safe training tray well clear of dry fuels and structures. @C01,C02,C14,C18
action: Stage full extinguishing water, stirring tool, eye/hand safety discipline and a dedicated safety observer BEFORE unpacking any ignition device or tinder. @C02,C04,T01,ED
action: Keep practice embers tiny, extinguish every trial completely cold in water before moving on and never assume backyard dry-day success guarantees wet winter field ignition. @C04,C12,ED
failure: Practicing ferro-rod sparks or bow-drill embers on a wooden porch, dry lawn or indoor garage floor without water staged. @C02,C05,C16
verify: Confirm legal permission, calm wind, mineral-soil/metal containment, abundant water and cold-to-touch sign-off at session close. @C01,C02,C04,ED
stop: Cancel or shut down training immediately if wind rises, humidity drops into Red Flag conditions, water runs low or any participant loses control of tools or flames. @C01,C04,C18
diagram: Authorized calm mineral site | Staged water & observer | Tiny trials -> drown cold
visual: Exploded training-site layout separating cleared burn pan, staged extinguishing water, observer position and wind/fuel clearance perimeter. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention and outdoor education

Z03-P45 | Distinguish conceptual ignition schematics from field-validated procedures | B008/B010 historical archive and editorial governance; new manuscript
objective: Interpret encyclopedia mechanism diagrams accurately without treating conceptual plates as guaranteed field ignition specs.
applies: All readers and reviewers evaluating traditional and modern ignition cards across Z03 and retained historical Track B plates.
equipment: Card evidence drawer, historical Track B crosswalk and local qualified instructor for any hands-on progression.
action: Separate conceptual mechanism illustrations (showing how components interact—such as flint-and-steel spark paths or bow-drill dust heating) from field-validated emergency procedures backed by agency safety standards. @C10,C12,ED
action: Preserve historical Track B plates (B007, B008, B010) in their separate frozen archive paths without misreading unscaled drawings or staged photos as species IDs, fixed dimensions or guaranteed ignition times. @C10,C12,ED
action: For real emergencies, prioritize verified modern redundant ignition (dry lighter, stormproof matches, ferro rod) paired with lawful site checks and a complete no-fire insulation plan. @C11,C01,C08
failure: Treating a conceptual bow-drill schematic as an easy zero-gear field substitute for carrying dry waterproof ignition and warm layers. @C12,C11,C08
verify: Check every card's evidence drawer and review footer to confirm whether a diagram depicts conceptual mechanics, agency safety rules or pending specialist review. @ED,C10,C12
stop: Never attempt an unpracticed historical ignition method as your sole warmth plan in cold or wet conditions; prioritize exposure control and emergency signaling. @C08,C12,T06
diagram: Conceptual mechanism plate | Agency safety procedure | Modern kit + no-fire fallback
visual: Decision tree distinguishing conceptual mechanism education from lawful agency safety rules and field-ready redundant ignition kits. Conceptual, not to scale.
reviewer: Fire prevention, editorial governance and mechanism reviewer
